An optional re-balance submod for [url=https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=$item-id$]Flavorium Universalis[/url].

Subscribe to this alongside the base mod if you want these balance tweaks. Skip it to keep vanilla culture capacity and literacy values.

[hr][/hr]

[h2]Culture Capacity[/h2]

Vanilla culture capacity bonuses are broadly reduced to make cultural integration a meaningful constraint.

[b]Advances[/b] — Cultural Acceptance, Vibrant Court, Persian in the Court, Millets: [b]1.0 → 0.5[/b] cultures capacity

[b]Government Reforms[/b] — French Feudal Nobility, Legacy of the Ottomans, Mughal Diwan, Legacy of Kublai Khan, Bohemian Commonwealth, Mandala System, Iroquois Reforged Law of Peace, Partitio Reform, Restored Rule of the Latins: reduced by 33–67%

[b]Laws[/b] — Allow Foreign Rituals: changed from flat [b]+1[/b] to [b]+20% cultures capacity modifier[/b]

[hr][/hr]

[h2]Literacy[/h2]

Literacy is class-stratified — nobles, clergy, and burghers have more avenues to high literacy than peasants, soldiers, and laborers.

[b]Advance overrides[/b]
[list]
[*] Written Alphabet: [b]global +5 → +2[/b]
[*] Printing Press: [b]global +10 → +5 nobles / +5 clergy / +5 burghers[/b]
[*] Innovativeness: [b]global +5 → +5 nobles / +5 clergy / +5 burghers[/b]
[/list]

[b]Library[/b] — class-split bonuses: [b]+3 nobles, +3 clergy, +2 burghers[/b] (replaces flat +5)

[b]Regional catch-up advances[/b] — four new advances provide partial offsets for Western Europe, Colonial powers, and East Asia

[hr][/hr]

[h2]Navy Rebalance[/h2]

Ship costs no longer balloon across the ages. Flat gold prices are unchanged — the runaway expense came from the market goods each hull demanded, which spiked hard in the Absolutism age. Those curves are now smoothed.

[list]
[*] [b]Light & transport ships[/b] — build-material cost grows by gentle, consistent increments with the Age-5 spike removed (light-ship lumber [b]4 → 6 → 8 → 11 → 14 → 18[/b] instead of …10 → [b]16[/b] → 20).
[*] [b]Heavy ships & galleys[/b] — rescaled to stay clearly the expensive hulls without the exponential late-game jump.
[*] [b]Maintenance[/b] — upkeep goods reduced and de-spiked for all four hull types; heavy ships remain the priciest to keep afloat.
[*] [b]Unique flagships[/b] — Ship of the Line and Cakradonya rescaled to track the new curves.
[/list]

[hr][/hr]

[h2]India — The Caste Order[/h2]

A social-mobility system for dharmic realms.

[list]
[*] [b]Brahmin Ritual Primacy[/b], [b]Kshatriya Martial Order[/b], [b]Jati Occupational Order[/b] — caste privileges granting order, martial steadiness and revenue at the cost of frozen social mobility.
[*] [b]The Varna Order[/b] reform codifies the hierarchy for an extra avenue of rule, trading away mobility and flexibility.
[*] [b]Reform the Caste Order[/b] event — loosen gradually, abolish caste outright, or uphold it; reform triggers a [b]Caste Upheaval[/b] settling into a [b]Reformed Social Order[/b].
[/list]

[hr][/hr]

[h2]China — The Imperial Examinations[/h2]

A bureaucratic-advancement system for keju realms.

[list]
[*] [b]Scholar-Gentry Examinations[/b] and [b]Examination Lineages[/b] — an able, studious administration whose narrow, costly path keeps office in gentry hands.
[*] [b]The Imperial Examinations[/b] reform makes the keju the spine of the state for an extra avenue of rule, throttling mobility and recruitment.
[*] [b]Reform the Examinations[/b] event — broaden the curriculum, abolish the exams, or keep them; reform triggers an [b]Examination Upheaval[/b] settling into a [b]Reformed Bureaucracy[/b].
[/list]

[hr][/hr]

[i]Requires: [b]Flavorium Universalis[/b] | Compatible with EU5 1.3.* | Multiplayer synchronized[/i]
